{
  "screens": [
    {
      "kind": "hookText",
      "text": "A birthday song can sound bright, while a sad film theme sounds dark. Even if you play both with the same starting note, changing one note in the group can change the mood.",
      "image": null,
      "visual": null
    },
    {
      "kind": "explanation",
      "title": "Change the middle note",
      "text": "A **chord** is notes played together. A half step moves to the next piano key, black or white; the root is the note you build on. From root C, E is four half steps up (the major third), and G is seven up (the fifth): C, E, G is a bright C major chord. Lower E one half step to E-flat (Eb): C, Eb, G is a darker C minor chord. To change major to minor, lower only the middle note one half step; keep the root and top note.",
      "exampleLineIdea": "choosing a chord for the mood of a song you play",
      "image": null,
      "visual": null,
      "explore": null
    },
    {
      "kind": "activity",
      "template": "keyboardFretboard",
      "content": "{\"template\":\"keyboardFretboard\",\"prompt\":\"Listen to the notes played together, then choose what you hear. The keys appear after you answer.\",\"fields\":{\"instruments\":[\"piano\"],\"target\":{\"kind\":\"chord\",\"quality\":\"minor\",\"root\":\"A\"},\"byEar\":true},\"check\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"question\":\"Did you hear a major or minor chord?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"minor\",\"text\":\"Minor\",\"isCorrect\":true,\"reason\":\"You heard A, C, and E together. C is the lowered middle note, giving the chord its darker sound.\"},{\"id\":\"major\",\"text\":\"Major\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"reason\":\"Major is tempting if you miss the lowered middle note. The chord played C between A and E, making it minor.\"}]}}"
    },
    {
      "kind": "check",
      "context": "You play C, E, and G together, then change the chord from major to minor.",
      "question": "Which note changes?",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "E drops to Eb.",
          "isCorrect": true,
          "reason": "You lower the middle note one half step and leave C and G in place.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "G drops to Gb.",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "reason": "G is the top note, so lowering it changes the wrong note. The middle note is E.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "C drops to B.",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "reason": "C is the root. Lowering it changes the note the chord is built on, not its middle note.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "E rises to F.",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "reason": "E is the middle note, but raising it goes the wrong way. You lower it one half step.",
          "picture": null
        }
      ],
      "image": null,
      "visual": null,
      "basis": {
        "screen": 2,
        "quote": "Lower E one half step to E-flat (Eb): C, Eb, G is a darker C minor chord."
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "check",
      "context": "Maya plays G, B, and D on her keyboard for a sad song, but the chord sounds too bright.",
      "question": "Which single key should she change, and why?",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Change B to Bb to lower the middle note.",
          "isCorrect": true,
          "reason": "You lower B one half step while keeping G and D. That turns the bright major chord into a darker minor chord.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Change D to Db to lower the top note.",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "reason": "Lowering D may seem like it would darken the sound, but D is the top note. The major-to-minor change happens in the middle.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Change G to Gb to lower the root.",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "reason": "G is the root, not the middle note. Changing it does not make G, B, D into G minor.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Change B to C to raise the middle note.",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "reason": "B is the middle note, but raising it goes the wrong way. To make this chord minor, you lower B.",
          "picture": null
        }
      ],
      "image": null,
      "visual": null,
      "basis": {
        "screen": 2,
        "quote": "To change major to minor, lower only the middle note one half step; keep the root and top note."
      }
    }
  ],
  "summary": [
    "A major chord has a middle note four half steps above its root and tends to sound bright.",
    "Lower that middle note one half step, keeping the root and top note, to make a darker minor chord."
  ]
}

{
  "screens": [
    {
      "kind": "hookText",
      "text": "Mendel crossed purple- and white-flowered pea plants, then grew and counted thousands of their seeds. White flowers disappeared in one generation, then came back in a strikingly regular share.",
      "image": null,
      "visual": null
    },
    {
      "kind": "explanation",
      "title": "Predicting white flowers",
      "text": "Each parent passes one version of its flower-color instruction to a seed. These versions are called **alleles**. Purple P masks white p: P is **dominant**, so Pp is purple and only pp is white. A **Punnett square** pairs each parent's possible letters; Pp × pp makes Pp, Pp, pp, and pp. Two of four squares are pp, predicting white flowers 1 in 2 times.",
      "exampleLineIdea": "a flower-color cross in a garden you help plant",
      "image": null,
      "visual": null,
      "explore": null
    },
    {
      "kind": "activity",
      "template": "combinationGrid",
      "content": "{\"template\":\"combinationGrid\",\"prompt\":\"Fill all four squares for a purple Pp plant crossed with a white pp plant.\",\"fields\":{\"title\":\"Purple Pp × white pp\",\"rule\":\"alleles\",\"rows\":{\"label\":\"Purple parent\",\"values\":[\"P\",\"p\"]},\"columns\":{\"label\":\"White parent\",\"values\":[\"p\",\"p\"]},\"traits\":{\"dominant\":\"Purple flowers\",\"recessive\":\"White flowers\"}},\"check\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"output\":\"recessive\",\"question\":\"What share of these seeds is predicted to grow white flowers?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"half\",\"text\":\"1 in 2\",\"value\":0.5,\"isCorrect\":true,\"reason\":\"You get pp in two of the four squares, so half are predicted to grow white flowers.\"},{\"id\":\"quarter\",\"text\":\"1 in 4\",\"value\":0.25,\"isCorrect\":false,\"reason\":\"You may remember 1 in 4 from Pp × Pp. Here the white parent can pass only p, leaving two pp squares.\"},{\"id\":\"all\",\"text\":\"All of them\",\"value\":1,\"isCorrect\":false,\"reason\":\"You may be looking only at the white parent's p letters. The purple parent can pass P, making two purple Pp squares.\"},{\"id\":\"none\",\"text\":\"None of them\",\"value\":0,\"isCorrect\":false,\"reason\":\"You may think purple always wins. But when the purple parent passes p, the seed gets pp and grows white flowers.\"}]}}"
    },
    {
      "kind": "activity",
      "template": "randomSimulation",
      "content": "{\"template\":\"randomSimulation\",\"prompt\":\"Guess how often exactly 50 flowers are white, then run 1,000 batches of 100 seeds.\",\"fields\":{\"mode\":\"chance\",\"model\":{\"kind\":\"binomial\",\"probability\":0.5,\"trials\":100,\"hit\":{\"comparison\":\"exactly\",\"value\":50}},\"runs\":1000,\"trialLabel\":\"One batch of 100 seeds\",\"hitLabel\":\"Exactly 50 white flowers\"},\"data\":{\"isExample\":true},\"check\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"question\":\"About what percentage of batches have exactly 50 white flowers?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"eight\",\"text\":\"About 8%\",\"value\":0.08,\"isCorrect\":true,\"reason\":\"You can expect about half the flowers to be white, but there are many counts near 50. Exactly 50 occurs about 8% of the time.\"},{\"id\":\"fifty\",\"text\":\"About 50%\",\"value\":0.5,\"isCorrect\":false,\"reason\":\"You may be treating a 1-in-2 chance for each seed as a 1-in-2 chance of getting exactly 50. Counts near 50 are common; that one exact count is not.\"},{\"id\":\"one\",\"text\":\"About 1%\",\"value\":0.01,\"isCorrect\":false,\"reason\":\"You may expect an exact count to be almost impossible. Exactly 50 is less common than a count near 50, but it still occurs about 8% of the time.\"},{\"id\":\"twentyfive\",\"text\":\"About 25%\",\"value\":0.25,\"isCorrect\":false,\"reason\":\"You may expect one batch in four to land exactly on the prediction. The batches spread across nearby counts, so only about 8% land on 50.\"}]}}"
    },
    {
      "kind": "check",
      "context": "In Maya’s Boise garden, 47 of 100 plants grown from seeds of a Pp × pp cross have white flowers.",
      "question": "Does this mean the 1-in-2 prediction was wrong?",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "No. The prediction is about 50 white flowers; 47 is close.",
          "isCorrect": true,
          "reason": "You use 1 in 2 to predict about 50 out of 100. Chance can give a nearby count such as 47.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Yes. Exactly 50 flowers must be white every time.",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "reason": "You may be reading 1 in 2 as a guaranteed count. Each seed has a chance of being white, so a batch need not land exactly on 50.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "Yes. This cross predicts only 25 white flowers.",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "reason": "You may be recalling the 1-in-4 result for Pp × Pp. This cross is Pp × pp, whose square has two white outcomes out of four.",
          "picture": null
        }
      ],
      "image": null,
      "visual": null,
      "basis": null
    }
  ],
  "summary": [
    "In a Pp × pp cross, two of four Punnett-square outcomes are pp, so the predicted share with white flowers is 1 in 2.",
    "A 1-in-2 chance predicts about 50 white flowers in 100 seeds, not exactly 50 in every batch."
  ]
}

