{
  "screens": [
    {
      "kind": "hookGuess",
      "question": "A Python instruction is meant to print 1 through 5, but prints only 1 through 4. Why might 5 be missing?",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Python stops before the ending number.",
          "isCorrect": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Python can print only four numbers at a time.",
          "isCorrect": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Python skips odd numbers after 1.",
          "isCorrect": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Python stops before the ending number. You'll see how a small change brings 5 back.",
      "image": null,
      "visual": null
    },
    {
      "kind": "explanation",
      "title": "Stop before the end",
      "text": "In Python, `range(start, stop)` starts at `start` and ends before `stop`: `range(1, 5)` gives 1, 2, 3, 4. To include 5, use `range(1, 6)`. The count of numbers is stop minus start: 5 − 1 = 4.",
      "exampleLineIdea": "numbering every page in a short packet without missing the last page",
      "image": {
        "prompt": "Draw a simple number line with evenly spaced numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6. Label the selection `range(1, 5)`. Highlight 1 through 4, mark 5 as left out, and leave 6 unhighlighted. Use clear, short English labels.",
        "alt": "A number line shows 1 through 4 highlighted for range(1, 5), with 5 left out and 6 unhighlighted."
      },
      "visual": null,
      "explore": null
    },
    {
      "kind": "check",
      "context": "You run `for n in range(2, 6): print(n)`.",
      "question": "What numbers does it print?",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "2, 3, 4, 5",
          "isCorrect": true,
          "reason": "You start at 2 and stop before 6, so 5 is the last number printed.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "2, 3, 4, 5, 6",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "reason": "Including 6 is tempting because it appears in the code, but 6 is the stop and is left out.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "3, 4, 5",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "reason": "Leaving out 2 treats the start like the stop. The start, 2, is included.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "2, 3, 4",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "reason": "Stopping at 4 leaves out 5 too early. Only the stop, 6, is left out.",
          "picture": null
        }
      ],
      "image": null,
      "visual": null,
      "basis": {
        "screen": 2,
        "quote": "In Python, `range(start, stop)` starts at `start` and ends before `stop`: `range(1, 5)` gives 1, 2, 3, 4."
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "activity",
      "template": "codeRunner",
      "content": "{\"template\":\"codeRunner\",\"prompt\":\"Edit only the loop line so it prints 1 through 5, one number per line.\",\"fields\":{\"language\":\"python\",\"fileName\":\"count.py\",\"starterCode\":\"for n in range(1, 5):\\n    print(n)\",\"editableLines\":[1],\"solution\":\"for n in range(1, 6):\\n    print(n)\",\"expectedOutput\":\"1\\n2\\n3\\n4\\n5\\n\",\"mistakes\":[{\"output\":\"0\\n1\\n2\\n3\\n4\\n\",\"feedback\":\"Starting at 0 adds 0 but still leaves out 5. Start at 1 and set the stop to 6.\"},{\"output\":\"1\\n2\\n3\\n4\\n\",\"feedback\":\"Leaving the stop at 5 leaves out 5. Set the stop to 6.\"}]},\"check\":{\"kind\":\"interaction\",\"explanation\":\"The loop starts at 1 and stops before 6, so it prints 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5.\"}}"
    },
    {
      "kind": "check",
      "context": "At a Seattle New Year's Eve party, a Python display runs `for n in range(10, 0, -1): print(n)`. The `-1` makes the numbers go down by one each time.",
      "question": "What is the last number printed?",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "1",
          "isCorrect": true,
          "reason": "You count down toward 0, but stop before reaching it. That makes 1 the last number printed.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "0",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "reason": "A countdown often ends at 0, so this is tempting. But 0 is the stop and is left out.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "10",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "reason": "10 is the first number printed. The `-1` keeps the loop counting down from there.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "-1",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "reason": "The `-1` tells Python how much to move each time; it is not the last number to print.",
          "picture": null
        }
      ],
      "image": null,
      "visual": null,
      "basis": {
        "screen": 2,
        "quote": "In Python, `range(start, stop)` starts at `start` and ends before `stop`: `range(1, 5)` gives 1, 2, 3, 4."
      }
    }
  ],
  "summary": [
    "`range(start, stop)` includes the start but leaves out the stop.",
    "To count from 1 through 5, use `range(1, 6)`; for an upward count, the number of values is stop minus start.",
    "The stop is left out when counting down, too."
  ]
}

